// Cycle of Life: Realistic Fauna — ARCHOSAUR! (archosaurfixd).
//
// One creature, the ferocidon: a 500 kg pack-hunting theropod that spawns in cool wet forest
// (0-20 C, rain 0.45+, forest 0.5+), hunts in the dark (maxLightLevel 7, block light only), climbs,
// and nests. It is fictional, so the numbers below are INVENTED — but anchored, and the anchors are
// written down so they can be argued with. The mod supplies the two that matter most on its own:
// weight (500 adult, 20 baby) and the climate band, so neither is guessed here.
//
// Life history is modelled on large archosaurs generally rather than on any one animal: slow to
// mature and long-lived like a crocodilian, laying a clutch that mostly does not survive. A 500 kg
// theropod is around the mass of a big saltwater crocodile, and growth studies on tyrannosaurids
// put mid-size theropods at roughly a decade to adult size and under thirty years of life, so that
// is the shape used — 25 years, a spring clutch, and 85% juvenile mortality.
//
// THREE CORRECTIONS, all of which this creature needs or it behaves nothing like its own design:
//
// 1. IT IS A PREDATOR, and it is detected as one — but only since Cycle of Life 0.15.25. Its hunt
//    list is written without domains ("bear-*", "wolf-*", "caninae-*"), which is the VANILLA
//    CONVENTION, not a mistake: the game matches entityCodes against an entity's code PATH and never
//    looks at the domain, and vanilla's own wolf hunts ["deer-*", "gazelle-*", "goat-*"] exactly that
//    way. Cycle of Life used to resolve those patterns against the predator's own domain, which
//    silently matched nothing for any MODDED predator. isPredator is stated here anyway: this entity
//    carries no tags at all, so nothing else would have caught it, and its food tags (meat, fish,
//    egg) would otherwise have made it a FORAGER — a 500 kg theropod grazing the small-prey pool.
//    (The mod's list does have two real slips: "deer-" is missing its wildcard, and "player" appears
//    twice. Neither matters to us.)
//
// 2. PREY BY MASS BAND, 10-600 kg, AND ITS OWN LIST DROPPED. Upper bound is a moose, the largest
//    thing on its wish list; lower bound keeps a half-tonne predator from chasing hares. The mod
//    wants it eating bears, wolves and big cats too, and now that domainless patterns resolve it
//    WOULD — so "preySpeciesCodes": [] clears the discovered list and leaves only the band.
//    Delete that line to hand its own list back.
//
//    Since 0.15.27 the band is no longer apex-blind: intraguild predation is gated on a MASS RATIO,
//    a third of the hunter's own weight by default. At 500 kg that gives the ferocidon an apex
//    ceiling near 165 kg — a wolf, a hyena, a leopard — and leaves lions, tigers and bears out of
//    reach. Which is close to the judgement that put this list aside in the first place: it could
//    probably take a wolf or a smaller big cat, and would not want to bet its life on anything more.
//
// 3. THE NEST IS A CHAIN SEED, and would not have been detected as one either. Nests spawn from
//    worldgen and runtime and hatch into ferocidons through the mod's own incubating -> mature ->
//    empty chain, which is self-capping and carries placement rules we do not reproduce. Chain
//    detection requires every spawnable variant to be STATIC, and it tests that against a known list
//    of stationary AI tasks — the nest has "bellalarm" alongside "idle", so it reads as mobile and
//    would have been managed as though it were a breeding animal: nests vetoed, nests spawned near
//    players, surplus nests despawned. Marked explicitly instead: observed, never spawn-controlled.
//
// The egg (ferocidon-egg-raw) needs no entry — its runtime chance is 0.0 for every type with no
// worldgen block, so it is never discovered. Same for tameferocidon, which has no spawn conditions
// at all: a tamed one is player property and outside the wild simulation entirely.
//
// VERIFICATION STATUS: keys derived from the mod's own entity JSON, not read off a live roster —
// this mod is not installed on the pack author's server. A wrong key is inert (the entry is ignored
// and the animal keeps its auto-discovered values); `/col species` lists the authoritative keys.
{
  "species": {

    // ===================== FEROCIDON =====================
    // K=1: the pack's floor, and right for an apex predator the mod itself spawns at 0.05 tries per
    // chunk. growthRate and predationVulnerability both sit below the big cats — it is heavier than
    // any of them and, by its own hunt list, afraid of nothing.
    "archosaur:ferocidon": {
      "carryingCapacity": 1,
      "isPredator": true,
      "preySpeciesCodes": [],            // drop the mod's own list (bears, wolves, big cats) — see 2
      "preyMaxBodyWeight": 600, "preyMinBodyWeight": 10,
      "preyPerPredator": 8,
      "growthRate": 0.08,
      "predationVulnerability": 0.02,
      // A modest sated floor, not zero: it hunts pigs, goats and sheep by design, and a half-tonne
      // predator in a paddock is the classic surplus-killing scenario. Well under the leopard's 0.3.
      "huntWhenFull": 0.1,
      "lifecycle": {
        "lifespanDays": 9000, "gestationDays": 90,
        "litterSizeMin": 2, "litterSizeMax": 5,
        "breedingMonths": [4, 5], "juvenileMortality": 0.85
      }
    },

    // ===================== NEST (chain seed) =====================
    // Observed, never spawn-controlled — see correction 3 above. Nothing else is tuned here: the
    // mod's own chain decides how many nests there are and what comes out of them.
    "archosaur:ferocidon-incubating": {
      "chainSeed": true
    }

  }
}
